[38;2;124;124;124m\delta_0 v_k - \xi_k \partial^\alpha \delta_0 v_j = 0$. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$\xi_j \partial^\alpha \delta_0 = -\alpha_j \partial^{\alpha - e_j} \delta_0$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(if $\alpha_j > 0$), or $0$ if $\alpha_j = 0$. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the condition becomes $-\alpha_j \partial^{\alpha-e_j}\delta_0 v_k + \alpha_k[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\partial^{\alpha - e_k}\delta_0 v_j = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $|\alpha| = 0$: $\hat{f} = \delta_0 v$, $\xi \times \hat{f} = 0$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mautomatically (since $\xi_j \delta_0 = 0$). And $f = $ constant vector $v$ (up [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mto FT constants), which is $\nabla(v \cdot x)$. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $|\alpha| = 1$, $\alpha = e_i$: $\hat{f} = \partial_{e_i}\delta_0 \cdot v$. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mCondition: $-\delta_{ij}\delta_0 v_k + \delta_{ik}\delta_0 v_j = 0$, i.e., $-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\delta_{ij}v_k + \delta_{ik}v_j = 0$ (as coefficients of $\delta_0$). For $j [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\neq i, k \neq i$: $0 = 0$. For $j = i, k \neq i$: $-v_k = 0$, so $v_k = 0$ for [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$k \neq i$. So $v = (0,...,v_i,...,0)$, i.e., $v = c \cdot e_i$. Then $\hat{f} =[0m[38;2;124;124;124mc \,\partial_{\xi_i}\delta_0 \, e_i$, and $f = c \,(-ix_i) e_i$ (up to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconstants), so $f = (-ic)(x_i) e_i$, which is $\nabla(-ic\, x_i^2/2)$. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis all checks out — no counterexample from point-supported distributions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mI'm confident: **the answer is yes**.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me write the clean proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m---[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Proof.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Yes**, such $u$ exists.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe use the Fourier transform $\mathcal{F}: \mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^3) \to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^3)$, which is an isomorphism. Recall that $\mathcal{F}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(\partial_j u) = i\xi_j \hat{u}$ and $\mathcal{F}((\text{curl}\, f)_i) = i(\xi [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\times \hat{f})_i$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe condition $\text{curl}\, f = 0$ translates to $\xi \times \hat{f} = 0$ in $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{S}'$, and we seek $\hat{u} \in \mathcal{S}'$ with $i\xi_j \hat{u} = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\hat{f}_j$ for $j=1,2,3$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Partition.** Choose $\chi \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^3)$ with $\chi \equiv 1$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mon $B(0,\epsilon)$ for some $\epsilon > 0$. Write $\hat{f} = \hat{f}^{(0)} + [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\hat{f}^{(\infty)}$ where $\hat{f}^{(0)} = \chi\,\hat{f}$ (compactly supported) [0m[38;2;124;124;124mand $\hat{f}^{(\infty)} = (1-\chi)\hat{f}$ (supported away from origin). Since $[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\chi$ is smooth, $\xi \times \hat{f}^{(0)} = \chi(\xi \times \hat{f}) = 0$ and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msimilarly $\xi \times \hat{f}^{(\infty)} = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 1 (away from origin).** Since $(1-\chi)$ vanishes near the origin, $|\xi|[0m[38;2;124;124;124m^{-2}$ is smooth on $\text{supp}(1-\chi)$. From $\xi \times \hat{f}^{(\infty)} =[0m[38;2;124;124;124m0$, the vector identity $\xi \times (\xi \times \hat{f}^{(\infty)}) = 0$ gives [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$|\xi|^2 \hat{f}^{(\infty)} = \xi\,(\xi \cdot \hat{f}^{(\infty)})$. Define[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\hat{u}^{(\infty)} := \frac{(1-\chi)\,(\xi \cdot \hat{f})}{i\,|\xi|^2} \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{S}'$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(product of the smooth slowly increasing function $(1-\chi)/(i|\xi|^2)$ with the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mtempered distribution $\xi \cdot \hat{f}$). Then[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$i\xi_j\,\hat{u}^{(\infty)} = \frac{(1-\chi)\,\xi_j\,(\xi\cdot\hat{f})}{|\xi|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m^2} = (1-\chi)\,\hat{f}_j = \hat{f}^{(\infty)}_j. \quad\checkmark$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 2 (near origin).** $\hat{f}^{(0)} = \chi\,\hat{f}$ is a compactly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msupported distribution. By the Paley–Wiener–Schwartz theorem, $g := \mathcal{F}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m^{-1}(\hat{f}^{(0)})$ is a smooth function with polynomial growth: $|g(x)| \leq [0m[38;2;124;124;124mC(1+|x|)^N$ for some $C, N$. Hence $g \in \mathcal{S}'$. Moreover, $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{curl}\, g = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(i\,\xi \times \hat{f}^{(0)}) = 0$, so $g$ is [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma classical smooth curl-free vector field.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the classical Poincaré lemma, define[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$v(x) := \int_0^1 g(tx)\cdot x\,dt.$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThen $v$ is smooth and $\nabla v = g$ (standard computation using $\partial_i [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mg_j = \partial_j g_i$). The growth estimate:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$|v(x)| \leq \int_0^1 |g(tx)|\,|x|\,dt \leq C|x|\int_0^1 (1+t|x|)^N\,dt \leq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mC'(1+|x|)^{N+1}$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mshows $v$ has polynomial growth, so $v \in \mathcal{S}'$. Setting $\hat{u}^{(0)}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m:= \hat{v}$, we have $i\xi_j\,\hat{u}^{(0)} = \hat{f}^{(0)}_j$. $\checkmark$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Conclusion.** Set $\hat{u} = \hat{u}^{(0)} + \hat{u}^{(\infty)} \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{S}'$ and $u = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(\hat{u})$. Then[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$i\xi_j\,\hat{u} = \hat{f}^{(0)}_j + \hat{f}^{(\infty)}_j = \hat{f}_j [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\quad\text{for } j=1,2,3,$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich gives $\nabla u = f$ in $\mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^3, \mathbb{R}^3)$. $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\blacksquare$[0m

